  ## Learning Objectives

  By the end of this module, you will be able to:

  - Apply "Start Undead" principle when creating new components
  - Calculate and maintain the Horde Ratio (≥7:1)
  - Navigate codebases using the 3-Click Brain Rule

  ## Rule 1: Start Undead

  ### The Rule

  ✅ **Components begin as pure zombies (no brains)**  
  ✅ **Add brains only when justified**  
  ❌ **Don't start with hooks/state on day one**

  ### Analogy: Building a House

  When building a house, you start with the foundation and structure before adding electrical wiring and plumbing. Similarly, start with the UI structure (zombie) before adding logic (brains).

  - **Foundation first**: Build the visual structure
  - **Wiring later**: Add logic only when you know what you need
  - **Avoid premature optimization**: Don't add complexity until necessary

  ### Why Start Undead?

  Starting with a pure component helps you:
  - Focus on the UI first
  - See what props you actually need
  - Avoid over-engineering
  - Make components easier to test

  ### Exercise: Refactor a Component

  Here's a component that started with brains. Refactor it to start undead:

  ```tsx
  // ❌ Started with brains
  function UserDashboard() {
    const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);
    const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
    const [error, setError] = useState(null);

    useEffect(() => {
      fetch('/api/users')
        .then(res => res.json())
        .then(data => {
          setUsers(data);
          setLoading(false);
        })
        .catch(err => {
          setError(err.message);
          setLoading(false);
        });
    }, []);

    if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;
    if (error) return <div>Error: {error}</div>;

    return (
      <div>
        <h1>Users</h1>
        <ul>
          {users.map(user => (
            <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    );
  }
  ```

  **Solution:**

  ```tsx
  // ✅ Start undead - pure zombie
  function UserDashboard({ users, loading, error }) {
    if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;
    if (error) return <div>Error: {error}</div>;

    return (
      <div>
        <h1>Users</h1>
        <ul>
          {users.map(user => (
            <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    );
  }

  // Brain boundary - handles logic
  function UserDashboardView() {
    const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);
    const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
    const [error, setError] = useState(null);

    useEffect(() => {
      fetch('/api/users')
        .then(res => res.json())
        .then(data => {
          setUsers(data);
          setLoading(false);
        })
        .catch(err => {
          setError(err.message);
          setLoading(false);
        });
    }, []);

    return (
      <UserDashboard
        users={users}
        loading={loading}
        error={error}
      />
    );
  }
  ```

  ### Quiz: Identify Which Should Start as Zombies

  Which of these components should start as zombies?

  1. `ProductCard` - Displays product information
  2. `ShoppingCart` - Manages cart state and calculations
  3. `NavigationBar` - Shows navigation links
  4. `PaymentForm` - Handles payment processing
  5. `Button` - Renders a button with text

  **Answer:** 1, 3, and 5 should start as zombies. 2 and 4 need brains from the start (but you can still start with a zombie structure and add brains).

